# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: cecil-native-arm64-runner — a native arm64 machine joined the fleet, and nothing can dispatch to it
#+STATUS: TODO
#+PRIORITY: P3
#+TYPE: infra
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: -

* What happened

On 2026-08-21 pjb registered two runners on *cecil*, an NVIDIA DGX Spark
running Ubuntu on aarch64:

: 55390921  cecil shell    tags: linux, aarch64, nvidia            run_untagged=true
: 55390973  cecil docker   tags: linux, aarch64, nvidia, docker    run_untagged=true

cecil is the fleet's first *native Linux arm64* machine. =release:linux:arm64=
is built on poseidon under *qemu-user emulation* today, which is the slowest
lane the project has.

* Why nothing runs on it yet

Runners are selected by =<os>,<arch>,<executor>= tags, and every job in
=.gitlab-ci.yml= names its tags explicitly. cecil says =aarch64= where the
project says =arm64=, and the shell runner carries no executor tag, so *no
job's tag set matches either runner*. cecil is online and idle.

That both runners accept untagged jobs is harmless for the same reason:
there is no untagged job in this project to mis-route onto the wrong
architecture. (It would stop being harmless the day someone adds a job that
overrides =tags:= with an empty list — the =default:= block otherwise supplies
=tags: [linux, amd64, docker]=.)

* The fix, in order of preference

1. *Retag the two runners to the convention* — =linux,arm64,shell= and
   =linux,arm64,docker=, keeping =nvidia= as a capability tag beside them
   (like =cad= / =gui=). cecil then serves the existing =linux,arm64,docker=
   configuration with *no change to =.gitlab-ci.yml= at all*. One edit in the
   GitLab UI. This is a decision for pjb, who owns the machine.

2. Failing that, teach the config the second spelling. There is no "either
   tag" operator — tag sets are AND-ed — so this means a second lane and a
   switch to choose between them, i.e. carrying the deviation forever in the
   file that documents the convention.

* Then: move release:linux:arm64 off qemu

Once (1) is done, =release:linux:arm64= can prefer cecil. The job body needs
*no change*: it already pulls =$ARM64_BASE=, the digest-pinned arm64
=buildpack-deps=, which is a native pull on cecil rather than a binfmt one.

The obstacle is that cecil is *intermittent*, like thalassa and the windows
PC, and the parent pipeline *cannot* gate on runner availability — GitLab
evaluates =rules:= at pipeline creation, before =detect:runners= has run
(the lesson of release-1.8.43, recorded at the top of =.gitlab-ci.yml=).
So the choice must be explicit, in the established style of
=SKIP_WINDOWS_RELEASE= / =ARM32_RELEASE=: keep qemu-on-poseidon as the
always-on default, and let a CI variable send the lane to cecil. Flip the
default the day cecil has proved itself over a few releases.

Worth measuring before and after: the emulated lane's duration is the whole
justification.

* What cecil does NOT solve

*arm32.* The DGX Spark's Grace cores are AArch64-only — the Cortex-X925 /
A725 generation dropped AArch32 at EL0 — so cecil cannot run armv7 code
natively any more than poseidon can. =release-arm32-thread-less-sbcl.issue=
still needs real 32-bit hardware (a Pi), or a threaded armhf SBCL.
This is reasoning from the CPU generation, not a probe: confirm it on the
machine (=cat /proc/cpuinfo=; try a =linux/arm/v7= container) before acting
on it either way.
